            "Gynaecological surgery ranges from a ten-minute procedure under local anaesthesia to a four-hour cancer operation, and the woman's preparation and recovery differ accordingly. <strong>Minor procedures</strong> — manual vacuum aspiration, endometrial biopsy, cervical biopsy and LEEP, Bartholin's marsupialisation, hysteroscopy and polypectomy, IUD insertion under anaesthesia, examination under anaesthesia — are day cases needing analgesia, a paracervical block or short general anaesthetic, a pregnancy test, and clear aftercare instructions. <strong>Diagnostic and operative laparoscopy</strong> — for ectopic pregnancy, ovarian cysts, endometriosis, tubal patency and sterilisation — uses carbon dioxide to inflate the abdomen through small incisions; the woman wakes with shoulder-tip pain from gas under the diaphragm (relieved by mobilising and analgesia, and worth warning her about), goes home within a day or two, and must know that persistent or worsening abdominal pain, fever, distension or vomiting after discharge may mean a bowel or vessel injury that was not apparent at the time. <strong>Laparotomy</strong> through a transverse Pfannenstiel incision (or a midline incision when the mass is large, the diagnosis is cancer or speed is needed) is the route of open myomectomy, abdominal hysterectomy, ovarian cystectomy and debulking surgery. <strong>Hysterectomy</strong> is <strong>total</strong> (uterus and cervix — the usual operation), <strong>subtotal</strong> (cervix left behind, so cervical screening continues), or <strong>radical</strong> (with the parametria, upper vagina and pelvic nodes, for cervical cancer), with or without <strong>bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy</strong>, and it is performed abdominally, vaginally (for prolapse, with the fastest recovery) or laparoscopically. <strong>Myomectomy</strong> preserves the uterus at the cost of more bleeding and adhesions. <strong>Prolapse repairs</strong> and continence procedures are vaginal operations in older women whose recovery is governed by the catheter, the bowels and the ban on straining. For each, the nurse's explanation covers what will be removed and what will remain — the single most common misunderstanding is that any ‘womb operation’ removes everything and ends womanhood — the incision, the catheter and drain she will wake with, how long she will stay, what she will feel, and when she can return to her work, her bed and her farm.",
