          paragraphs: [
            "<strong>Gender-based violence</strong> is violence directed at a person because of their gender or that affects one gender disproportionately; <strong>sexual violence</strong> is any sexual act or attempt obtained by coercion; and <strong>intimate partner violence</strong> — physical, sexual, psychological and economic abuse by a current or former husband or partner — is the commonest form, experienced by roughly one Ghanaian woman in three over her lifetime, with surveys in Ghana finding that a quarter or more of women report some form of domestic violence in the past year alone. Other forms the nurse meets are rape and defilement, sexual harassment and exploitation, trafficking, forced and child marriage, female genital mutilation, widowhood rites, accusations of witchcraft that drive older women into camps in the north, and violence against women with disabilities, sex workers and women who love women, who are least able to report it. Violence rarely presents as violence. It presents as the woman with injuries that do not match the story or are at different stages of healing, with bruises on the inner arms, breasts, abdomen and genitals rather than the shins; as repeated visits for headaches, chronic pelvic pain, ‘waist pains’, insomnia, depression or anxiety; as recurrent sexually transmitted infections, unintended pregnancy and requests for abortion or emergency contraception; as the woman who is never allowed to attend alone, whose partner answers for her, who cannot use contraception because he forbids it, who misses appointments and defaults from HIV treatment; as the pregnant woman booking late with a vaginal bleed or abdominal injury; and as the girl under 16 who is pregnant. Every one of these is a reason to ask about safety, in private, and the first-line response is <strong>LIVES</strong>: <strong>L</strong>isten without judgement, <strong>I</strong>nquire about her needs and concerns, <strong>V</strong>alidate — tell her she is believed, that it is not her fault and that no one deserves to be hit — <strong>E</strong>nhance her safety with a plan, and <strong>S</strong>upport her with information and referral. The nurse does not tell her to leave, confront the partner, or report against her wishes; she documents, treats, offers the pathway, and keeps the door open, because most women leave only after several attempts and the clinic that received her kindly is where she will return.",
          ],
