          alt: "Traditional practices: sort them before you judge them. Most are harmless, some are helpful, a few cause the injury you are treating. Harmless, leave them alone: naming ceremonies and rites, prayer, blessing, holy water, beads and protective threads, herbal baths for a well baby, traditional foods in pregnancy, massage of an aching back — opposing these costs you the trust you need for the rest. Negotiable, keep but modify: birth attended at home, bone setting for a simple fracture, herbal treatment of minor illness, confinement and rest after birth — agree the danger signs and the route to the facility. Harmful, act and report: cutting the uvula or the gum buds, anything applied into a baby's eye, dressings on the umbilical cord, enemas and purgatives in infants, shared unsterile cutting blades, chaining or beating a mentally ill person — these injure, infect or kill; female cutting is a crime. Ask what the practice is meant to achieve and offer a safer way to reach the same aim; replace the method, keep the meaning. When the patient arrives injured by a practice, treat the injury first.",
