            "<strong>Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)</strong> — growth of the transition zone of the prostate under the influence of dihydrotestosterone with age — affects half of men over 60 and most over 80, and because the prostate surrounds the urethra it produces <strong>lower urinary tract symptoms</strong>: the <strong>voiding</strong> symptoms of hesitancy, a weak intermittent stream, straining, terminal dribbling and a feeling of incomplete emptying, and the <strong>storage</strong> symptoms of frequency, urgency, urge incontinence and <strong>nocturia</strong>, which is usually what finally brings a man to the clinic. The assessment is the history and the <strong>International Prostate Symptom Score</strong> (seven questions scored 0 to 5, with a quality-of-life question), a drug history (anticholinergics, decongestants and some antidepressants precipitate retention; diuretics worsen frequency), a <strong>digital rectal examination</strong> — the benign prostate is smoothly enlarged, firm and rubbery with a preserved median sulcus; a hard, irregular, nodular or asymmetric gland suggests cancer — urinalysis for infection and blood, serum creatinine, a <strong>prostate-specific antigen (PSA)</strong> after counselling, a frequency-volume chart, and a post-void residual by ultrasound or catheter. Complications are acute and chronic retention, recurrent urinary infection, bladder stones, haematuria, overflow incontinence and bilateral hydronephrosis with renal failure in the man who has been silently retaining for years. Mild symptoms are managed by reassurance, reducing evening fluids, caffeine and alcohol, timed and double voiding, and treating constipation; moderate symptoms by an <strong>alpha-blocker</strong> (tamsulosin 400 micrograms at night, which relaxes the smooth muscle of the prostate and bladder neck within days — warn of dizziness on standing, retrograde ejaculation and nasal stuffiness, and give the first dose at bedtime) and, for a large gland, a <strong>5-alpha-reductase inhibitor</strong> (finasteride 5 mg daily, which shrinks the prostate over six months, halves the PSA — the laboratory value must be doubled when interpreting — and causes reduced libido and erectile dysfunction in some; women who are or may become pregnant must not handle crushed tablets); and severe or complicated symptoms by surgery — <strong>transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP)</strong>, open prostatectomy for very large glands, or newer laser and minimally invasive procedures where available.",
            "<strong>Acute urinary retention</strong> — the sudden painful inability to pass urine with a tender, distended, dull bladder — follows BPH, constipation, infection, alcohol, cold weather, anaesthesia and the drugs above, and in a woman follows a retroverted gravid uterus, a prolapse, a haematocolpos or a pelvic mass. The treatment is <strong>urethral catheterisation</strong> with the male technique of the diagram in lesson 5.2 — penis held upright, generous lidocaine gel left for a few minutes, the catheter advanced to the bifurcation before the balloon is inflated, the foreskin replaced — or <strong>suprapubic catheterisation</strong> by a trained clinician when the urethra cannot be passed; the volume drained is recorded (over about 1 litre suggests chronic retention); the catheter is <strong>not clamped intermittently</strong>, since rapid decompression causes only transient haematuria and the old practice of slow drainage has no benefit; and the man is watched for <strong>post-obstructive diuresis</strong> — more than 200 mL an hour for several hours after relief of chronic retention — with fluid and electrolyte replacement and a check of his creatinine, because he may have been in post-renal kidney injury for weeks. An alpha-blocker is started and a <strong>trial without catheter</strong> arranged after two to three days; a man who fails the trial goes home with a catheter or learns intermittent self-catheterisation and is listed for surgery.",
