            "<strong>Prostate cancer</strong> is, with liver cancer, the leading cancer of Ghanaian men — about <strong>2,400 new cases a year</strong> in GLOBOCAN's 2022 estimate, certainly an undercount — and men of West African ancestry have among the highest incidence and mortality in the world, presenting younger and with more aggressive disease than European men. Risk rises steeply with <strong>age</strong> (most cases after 65), with a <strong>family history</strong> in a father or brother (two- to three-fold) and with inherited mutations such as BRCA2; diet and obesity contribute. It arises in the peripheral zone, so early disease causes <strong>no symptoms</strong> — lower urinary tract symptoms, when they come, are usually from coexisting BPH — and advanced disease presents with haematuria, haemospermia, erectile dysfunction, <strong>bone pain in the back, hips and pelvis</strong>, pathological fracture, spinal cord compression (back pain with leg weakness and retention — an emergency), weight loss, anaemia and renal failure from ureteric obstruction. Detection rests on the <strong>digital rectal examination</strong> (a hard, irregular, nodular or asymmetric gland with loss of the median sulcus) and the <strong>PSA</strong>, a protein made by the prostate that rises with cancer but also with BPH, infection, retention, catheterisation, ejaculation and vigorous cycling, so a raised value (above 4 ng/mL, or lower age-adjusted thresholds) is repeated after a few weeks before anything else is done and is interpreted with the examination, the man's age and his finasteride use. Whether to screen asymptomatic men with PSA is debated — it finds some cancers that would never have harmed the man and leads to biopsies and treatments with real side effects, while in high-risk populations like Ghanaian men it finds the aggressive cancers earlier — and the honest counselling before a PSA test says all of this and lets the man decide; most Ghanaian urologists advise discussion of testing from 40 to 45 in men with a family history and from 45 to 50 in others. Diagnosis is by <strong>transrectal or transperineal biopsy</strong> under antibiotic cover (the nurse explains the procedure, the rectal bleeding and haematuria that follow, and the sepsis that must bring him back), with the <strong>Gleason grade</strong> describing aggressiveness, and staging by MRI, bone scan and PSA level. Treatment depends on stage, grade, age and the man's wishes: <strong>active surveillance</strong> for low-risk disease in older men; <strong>radical prostatectomy</strong> or <strong>radical radiotherapy</strong> (external beam or brachytherapy) for localised disease, with incontinence and erectile dysfunction as the trade-offs that must be discussed; and for advanced and metastatic disease, <strong>androgen deprivation</strong> — bilateral orchidectomy (cheap, immediate and widely used in Ghana) or GnRH analogue injections, with anti-androgens, abiraterone or chemotherapy added as the disease becomes castration-resistant — plus bisphosphonates and radiotherapy for bone pain and dexamethasone and urgent radiotherapy for cord compression. Androgen deprivation causes hot flushes, loss of libido and erections, fatigue, gynaecomastia, osteoporosis, weight gain and depression, and the man needs to hear this beforehand and be supported through it; the nurse who talks to men's groups about prostate awareness, examines the prostate of the older man with ‘waist pains’ and arranges the PSA before the back pain becomes paraplegia changes the stage at which Ghanaian men are diagnosed.",
